= [+ royalist_leader +] and Goodryke

In [+ royalist_leader +]'s pursuit of power, a certain champagne salesman by the name of Joachim von Carlile has offered to act as an intermediary to former Chancellor von Goodryke, whom he met during the Great War. [? if lippe_election_seen: With the High Cross campaign now over, ?][+ royalist_leader +] finally has time to resume talks with Goodryke—who is more than eager himself.

Accompanied by Torin Brocke and [? if not rohm_explode: Isen Glade?][? if rohm_explode: the leader of the <span style="color: #7A3C00;">**Loyalists**</span>?], [+ royalist_leader +] joined Goodryke for lunch at Carlile’s villa, reiterating his demand for the chancellorship. He claimed that the <span style="color: #7A3C00;">Royalist</span> movement was still very much alive, and could not accept a subordinate role in any Goodryke-led cabinet.

Goodryke pushed back, insisting that President DuFour’s opposition to appointing [+ royalist_chancellor +] as Chancellor remained firm—and that even he could not change the old man's mind. The meeting ended without resolution, and with no plans to meet again. Despite increased secrecy, some of the details still leaked to the press.

- @Sovereigntist: [+ royalist_chancellor +] remains far from the chancellorship!

@Sovereigntist

When Staal was asked for comment following the leaks, his illusions about Goodryke’s intentions were finally shattered: he at last realizes that his old friend is scheming against him.

The chancellor has exhausted his possible avenues. The <span style="color: #7A3C00;">**Royalists**</span> has no interest in tolerating him, with Crowde powerless, and DuFour increasingly unlikely to grant an emergency decree to postpone elections. Having alienated all political parties in the Assembly, he is truly doomed in a vote of no confidence.

Despite his military training, Staal has committed a cardinal mistake: he failed to prepare an avenue of retreat in the event that his plans collapsed. He is still banking on [+ royalist_leader +] changing his mind—and if that fails, on DuFour’s willingness to support his election postponement.

Staal has given no thought to adopting a defensive posture; instead, he continues to pursue his offensive strategy as if nothing has changed.